# Rater: Developer Onboarding & Backend Architecture Guide

Welcome to the **Rater** engineering team! This document serves as your complete guide to onboarding into the backend architecture, codebase patterns, and database systems of Rater.

Rater is a high-performance, aesthetically curated design review platform where creatives submit their work and receive structured, multi-dimensional community feedback across three key metrics: **Clarity**, **Purpose**, and **Aesthetics**.

---

## 1. System Architecture Overview

Rater is built on a modern **Next.js 16 (App Router)** frontend utilizing **React 19** and **Tailwind CSS 4**. The architecture is explicitly designed to decouple the client presentation layer from the data persistence layer.

### The Decoupled State Flow

Currently, the application runs on a client-side mock database with local storage persistence. The state layer is designed with clean boundaries, making the transition to a real backend isolated and safe.

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph Frontend [Presentation & UI Layer]
        UI[React Components & Hooks]
        Zustand[Zustand navigationStore]
    end

    subgraph State [Application State Seam]
        AuthCtx[AuthContext.tsx]
        PostCtx[PostContext.tsx]
        TimeCtx[TimeContext.tsx]
    end

    subgraph Mock [Current Mock Backend]
        MockDB[mockData.ts]
        LS[Browser LocalStorage]
    end

    subgraph Prod [Target Production Backend]
        SupaAuth[Supabase Auth]
        SupaDB[Supabase DB / PostgreSQL]
        Cloudinary[Cloudinary Media CDN]
    end

    UI -->|1. Invokes Actions| AuthCtx
    UI -->|1. Invokes Actions| PostCtx
    Zustand -->|Handles Ephemeral UI State| UI
    
    %% Current Flow
    AuthCtx -.->|2a. Current Mock Flow| MockDB
    PostCtx -.->|2a. Current Mock Flow| MockDB
    MockDB -.->|3a. Read/Write| LS

    %% Target Flow
    AuthCtx ===>|2b. Production Flow| SupaAuth
    PostCtx ===>|2b. Production Flow| SupaDB
    UI ===>|2c. Direct Media Upload| Cloudinary
