## 3. Database Schema & Relationships (Supabase)

The production PostgreSQL database in Supabase is normalized to handle scalable user actions, media associations, and structured metrics.

### Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD)

```mermaid
erDiagram
    auth_users ||--|| profiles : "syncs to (1:1)"
    profiles ||--o{ posts : "creates (1:N)"
    profiles ||--o{ reviews : "writes (1:N)"
    posts ||--o{ reviews : "has (1:N)"
    posts ||--o{ badges : "awarded (1:N)"
    categories ||--o{ posts : "classifies (1:N)"

    profiles {
        uuid id PK "FK to auth.users"
        text username UNIQUE
        text email UNIQUE
        text name
        text role
        text avatar_url
        text bg_color
        text bio
        boolean is_blocked
        jsonb social_links
    }

    posts {
        uuid id PK
        uuid avatar_id FK "References profiles.id"
        text title
        text description
        text category FK "References categories.name"
        text image_url
        boolean is_deleted
        timestamptz deleted_at
    }

    reviews {
        uuid id PK
        uuid post_id FK "References posts.id"
        uuid reviewer_id FK "References profiles.id (Nullable)"
        text reviewer_name "For guest ratings"
        text device_id "For anonymous tracking"
        integer clarity
        integer purpose
        integer aesthetics
        text comment
    }
```

### DB Triggers & Calculated Metrics

#### 1. Automatic Profile Generation
To streamline registration and avoid secondary user profile insertion scripts on the client, we deploy a database trigger in PostgreSQL. When a user registers through Supabase Auth, the following trigger syncs their information from `auth.users` to our public `profiles` table:

```sql
create or replace function public.handle_new_user()
returns trigger as $$
begin
  insert into public.profiles (id, username, email, name, bg_color)
  values (
    new.id,
    coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'username', 'user_' || substring(new.id::text from 1 for 8)),
    new.email,
    coalesce(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'name', 'New Member'),
    '#FEC312'
  );
  return new;
end;
$$ language plpgsql security definer;

create trigger on_auth_user_created
  after insert on auth.users
  for each row execute procedure public.handle_new_user();
```

#### 2. Relational Aggregate View
To calculate metrics dynamically on the fly without running heavy JOIN operations inside our React components, we utilize a database view:

```sql
create or replace view post_metrics as
select 
  p.id as post_id,
  count(r.id) as review_count,
  coalesce(round(avg((r.clarity + r.purpose + r.aesthetics) / 3.0), 1), 0) as average_score,
  (count(r.id) >= 3) as rating_unlocked
from posts p
left join reviews r on p.id = r.post_id
group by p.id;
```
*Note: A post's aggregate stats remain "locked" (`rating_unlocked = false`) until a minimum of 3 ratings are collected, protecting creators from early outliers.*

